    "created_at": "2026-10-06T14:25:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-06T14:25:09Z",
    "body": "From what I understand, `object-fit` makes the concrete size of the image depend on two things:\n\na) The width/height of the container (which can be defined by the image's width/height attributes)\nb) The *intrinsic* aspect ratio of the image (to account for `cover` and `contain` etc)\n\n(b) can only be derived from an image that is already fetched.\n\nThis creates a circular dependency - as re-fetching the image might bring an image with a different aspect ratio. I don't think this re-fetching is spec'ed. So implementations mostly short-circuit this and assume that the image's width/height attributes also define the aspect ratio.\n\nAre we recommending that authors use the natural width/height (or at least the natural aspect ratio) in the attributes and then correct it to the displayed size with CSS?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
