    "body": "> WDYT?\n> \n> cc [@austinh](https://github.com/austinh)\n\n+1, this would solve a real problem for us. At Airbnb most listing photos render with object-fit: cover in fixed-ratio boxes, and the photos are usually wider than the box. Content-box selection alone under-fetches: a 4:3 photo in a 1:1 card is drawn at 1.33x the box width, so it looks soft. A fixed per-image correction doesn't hold up either, because the box's aspect ratio changes across breakpoints and photos in the same grid have different ratios. `w/h` lets the UA work it out from the real layout. Our CDN/backend already knows source dimensions when it builds srcset, so emitting h costs us nothing.\n\nSome thoughts:\n\n* The hint wins after load: if the fetched image's real ratio differs from w/h, don't re-select. That keeps selection independent of image content and rules out loops. It's probably also worth saying whether w/h are in post-EXIF-orientation terms.\n* Eager images: this only helps sizes=auto. Above-the-fold images with explicit sizes are chosen by the preload scanner before layout, so they'd still under-fetch with cover. Is there anything we could do there, e.g. letting authors declare the box ratio up front? (this is where my `densityscale` proposal comes in handy)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
